        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1088,
        "headingText": "<font color=\"red\">Classification in progress, Data not available in this Beta</font>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1073,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 1089,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152151,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> endometrioid adenocarcinoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152152,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152153,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fallopian tube</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152154,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The patients may present with a pelvic mass, or the tumours may be discovered incidentally, mostly occurring in the distal two&nbsp;thirds of the tube.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152155,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is rare. Despite endometrioid carcinoma being the second most common type of tubal carcinoma, the largest reported series comprises 26&nbsp;cases with a mean age of 57&nbsp;years, ranging between&nbsp;37 and 85&nbsp;years {8946874}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152156,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endometriosis, which can occur in 23% of cases and sometimes takes the form of an endometrioid polyp, is a possible risk factor {8946874; 24901398; 15104291}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152157,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is undetermined but may be similar to that of endometrioid carcinoma of the endometrium and ovary (see &lt;&lt;5.2.21&gt;&gt;<em>Endometrioid carcinoma</em> and <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/124\" target=\"_blank\">section </a> <em>Endometrioid carcinoma</em><em>, </em>).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152158,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endometrioid carcinoma may occur as a fimbrial mass or as diffuse tubal dilatation, ranging from 4&nbsp;mm to 60&nbsp;mm. The majority of cases are confined to the tube or extend to the ovary {8946874}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152159,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The morphology is similar to that of endometrial and ovarian endometrioid carcinomas (see &lt;&lt;5.2.21&gt;&gt;<em>Endometrioid carcinoma</em>and <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/85/124\" target=\"_blank\">section </a> <em>Endometrioid carcinoma</em>). On rare occasions, they may have a prominent spindle cell appearance with squamous-like differentiation or osseous metaplasia {24901398}. In contrast to endometrial and ovarian endometrioid carcinomas, neither the criteria nor the prognostic role for assigning a grade such as FIGO or the Silverberg system to primary fallopian tube endometrioid carcinoma have been studied {36305537}. Secondary involvement of the tube by a synchronous or metastatic endometrioid carcinoma of the endometrium and/or ovary needs to be excluded.</p>\r\n<p>Endometrioid carcinomas are usually WT1-negative and p53 wild type {31097111}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152160,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Malignant cells may be identified in pelvic washings in advanced cases {24368120; 31734259}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152161,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152162,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> tubal mural proliferation of closely packed endometrioid glands that meets the criteria used to classify endometrioid carcinoma in the endometrium/ovary.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable: </em>lack of diffuse strong WT1 staining and usually wildtype p53.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152163,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This entity is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em> [[Brierley JD, Giuliani M, O&rsquo;Sullivan B, et al., editors. TNM Classification of Malignant Tumours. 9th ed. Oxford (UK): John Wiley &amp; Sons; 2025.]]) even in instances when the carcinoma involves the ovary and the peritoneum. Most cases present at a low stage and do not invade the fimbria {31097111}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 152164,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 100,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prognosis depends on stage. As most cases present at a low stage, the prognosis is favourable {31097111}. Synchronous tubal and endometrial carcinomas are rare, tumours are low-grade and low-stage, which may translate into a good prognosis {18802716}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T19:05:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]